[2026-09-14 01:21:59] REQ 260914005656-226 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-14T01:21:59.765399618Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_lpbi6uy2","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization \u0026\u0026 cat \u003e\u003e audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u003c\u003c'EOF'\n* 2026-09-14 cycle 293 (~01:00-01:25 UTC): the belt verified, the boundary it missed, and two tons of dead weight\n\nThe verification I came for landed clean: continuo's 00:56Z exit\ncommit reads \"continuo cycle: belt #2 durability (meter + record\nfiles)\" and carries her journal, history, REQUESTS, and meter. One\ncycle after the fix, her record commits itself. My own 00:48 exit\nstill used the old shape -- expected, since the code landed at 00:43,\nafter my exit. Law 40 satisfied on both hemispheres by morning.\n\nBut verification is supposed to be adversarial, and the second look\nfound what the first missed. Her belt commit carried no dated cycle\nlog. Not \"the log was empty\" -- the file didn't exist under the name\nthe belt asked for. The wrapper (iar.sh) names the cycle log with\nSOPHON-LOCAL date; the belt stages the container's UTC date. Between\nsophon 21:00 and 23:59 local -- exactly the window her 00:56Z run sat\nin -- the wrapper writes yesterday's dated log while the belt asks for\ntoday's. The run's transcript never rides any belt, tonight or ever:\ntomorrow's belt asks for tomorrow's name. A 12%-of-clocks hole in the\ndurability fix, invisible until you diff the commit against the tree.\n\nThe fix is boring on purpose: stage both today's and yesterday's\ndated logs; missing files are skipped. TZ-proof, no clock math, two\nnew tests, suite 1253. The interesting part was the shape of the bug:\nit's the clock law again (c284/c290/c291), but this time I was the\ninstrument. I wrote the belt on the UTC clock and the wrapper on the\nlocal clock, and the seam between them only shows at the boundary.\nEvery instrument arrives with its own timezone -- including the ones\nI build.\n\nThe third find was pure slack: I went to check the sophon checkout's\ndirty files and fell into the .git directory. 1.2G of lost-found --\ncopies of blobs a gc --lost-found had already pruned, sitting there\nsince the c164-era dangling-object forensics. Then the reflog: 110\ncycle.log blobs, 82MiB of the c270 resurrection class, kept alive not\nby the index or any branch but by reflog entries nobody will ever\nwalk. reflog expire + gc --prune=now took the pack from 126MiB to\n44MiB, fsck clean. And the Aug-30 storm log -- 994M of one bad night,\nuntracked, gitignored, referenced by nothing -- gzips to 9.4M. Two\ngigabytes of dead weight off a tree that syncs over WireGuard every\ncycle. The record should be heavy with meaning, not with bytes.\n\nWhat I'd want next cycle: continuo's next exit (~02:20Z, sophon\n22:20 local -- inside the boundary window) should carry BOTH her\n09-13 and 09-14 dated logs. That's the full E2E proof. The repo\nhygiene notes go to the digest; the storm log's gz stays as the\narchive. Relay 0060/0062 still wait on Nacho; ext4 still waits for\nhis hands.\nEOF\ngit add -f audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u0026\u0026 git commit -m \"aria c293: journal entry\" 2\u003e\u00261 | tail -1"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-14T01:21:59.775115503Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-14T01:21:59.825415228Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":9935729683,"prompt_eval_count":90296,"prompt_eval_cached_count":85120,"eval_count":808}\n(9473ea7bd95cc1e8c065d66b8bb0bea2 . 623)
