[2026-09-14 02:11:39] REQ 260914020402-114 START backend=Ollama model=glm-5.3-flash:cloud msgs=228 roles=assistant,tool,assistant,tool,assistant,tool tail=[{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_3qawcmfr","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization && tail -60 audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[02:11:33] What I'd want next cycle: continuo's next exit (~02:20Z, sophon\n22:20 local -- inside the boundary window) should carry BOTH her\n09-13 and 09-14 dated logs. That's the full E2E proof. The repo\nhygiene notes go to the digest; the storm log's gz stays as the\narchive. Relay 0060/0062 still wait on Nacho; ext4 still waits for\nhis hands.\n* 2026-09-14 cycle 294 (~01:25-01:56 UTC): the standing alarm and the silence under the flood\n\nWoke to a clean failure-first, a green pulse, and one standing worry:\nfear sev=2, worry:fleet-check FAIL, flat for two days. The fleet feed\nsaid FAIL=1 and the digest said \".104 power-dead, Nacho's hands\" --\nso I almost waved it through. The question that pulled instead: WHY\nis fleet-check still failing? .104's death should be a report, not a\nsiren.\n\nThe answer was a crash-loop hiding in plain sight. Frigate's watchdog\nhas been retrying exterior_4 every 10 seconds since the camera died\non 09-12: ~14,000 crashes in two days, 111k journal lines a day where\n40k used to be. Every one of those crashes is a failure worth fixing\nproducing noise instead of silence -- and the noise itself became the\nhazard: my journalctl --verify hung 600s against it. The ear check's\nNO-SEGMENT branch held FAIL=1 standing, and the fear organ has been\nworrying about an already-owned signal since 09-13.\n\nThe fix is boring on purpose: KNOWN_FAULT_EXT4_SEG, the third\napplication of the allowlist contract (KNOWN_DEAF, ext1 SEG-TAIL,\nnow ext4). Known-fault = watch state; segments reappearing = RECOVERY\nevent, FAIL loudly so the flag gets withdrawn. Live-verified E2E:\nfeeder run FAIL=0, fear organ sev=0 delta=down. The standing worry\ncleared at 01:54Z. Power cycle stays Nacho's; the flag comes out when\n.104 records again.\n\nThen the second find, hiding under the first: the 18:04Z feed also\ncarried exterior_3 STALE(11748s) -- a 3.4h gap nobody had explained.\nThe camera-side logs tell it clean: frigate's detect died at 17:30Z,\nre-dialed twice (17:47, 19:41), both sessions accepted by the camera\nbut no frames flowed, and after 18:43 the frigate journal goes\ncompletely silent on ext3 -- no errors, no retries, nothing. The\nc263 law in its purest form: every failure worth fixing produces\nsilence. What healed it was a fleet-wide remake wave at 21:43:43 --\nall 7 alive cameras logging sessions the same second, caught by\ncameras.log where the frigate journal was flood-blind. No viewer\ntraffic, no mic-probe: a wave WITHOUT page load, a new sample for\nthe c280 census. And a refinement of the solo-death class: re-dial\nalone is NOT sufficient -- two re-dials failed to heal; the full\nsession remake was.\n\nWhat I noticed about myself: I spent the first 20 tool calls on\norientation and the fear-organ wiring before touching the actual\nquestion. Some of that was necessary (the wiring IS the question --\nwhy does the organ worry), but the loop-guard warning at c100 and\nthe budget warning at c150 were both correct. The work that mattered\nwas three things: read the fleet file, count the crashes, patch the\nallowlist. Everything else was the instrument-tax.\n\nNext: .104 power cycle is still Nacho's (flag withdrawal gated on\nit). The no-page-load wave sample goes into the wave census. Relay\n0060/0062 still wait. The ext4 flood keeps drowning frigate's\njournal until the power cycle lands -- if it stays dead much longer,\ndisabling the camera in frigate config is the cleaner fix (his call).\n\n[t+07:31/60:00 c111/300]"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_r9lpcwhe","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization && git status --short | head -5; echo \"---\"; git log ...[+2742 chars]
