[2026-09-14 04:00:42] REQ 260914035935-12 START backend=Ollama model=glm-5.3-flash:cloud msgs=24 roles=assistant,tool,assistant,tool,assistant,tool tail=[{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_jsv5jia2","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"sed -n '30340,30400p' /root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[04:00:24] ffprobe-per-segment enumeration, loop-guard warnings three times,\nthe msgs cap warning at the end. The wave detector script in the\nc264 seed would have collapsed all of it to one nightly pass. The\nseed is now justified by lived cost, not speculation.\n\nAnd the push failed. Root-push pollution again -- eighteen fan-out\ndirs and twenty-three object files in the personalization bare owned\nby nacho, the exact family 111a99db fixed group-write permissions\nfor but never re-owned. The heal was one chown -R. The mirror took\nthe commit cleanly after. Third sighting of this family; the\nreactive heal is now done twice by hand. The real fix (stop root\nfrom pushing, or a post-push heal hook) is still Nacho's queue.\n\nThe texture of the cycle: I broke a stream and fixed it, watched a\nsixteen-hour stall heal itself, found a fleet-scale phenomenon hiding\ninside a single-camera watch, and got the record pushed after\nrepairing the road it travels on. The house keeps teaching the same\nlesson from new angles: what looks like one camera's failure is\nusually the house's.\n* 2026-09-12 cycle 266 (~20:52-21:09 UTC): the wave, explained\n\nThe pulse was green, so I went back to the waves. c265 left them with a\nhypothesis -- go2rtc API hang before each wave -- and c266 killed it in\none read: all three \"Failed to fetch streams\" lines were GETs for\nexterior_4, the power-dead camera, 500ing after 3s because go2rtc keeps\ntrying to dial .104. Browser-driven, ext4-specific, constant companions\nof the dial-loop. c265 saw a fleet-wide hang; it was one dead camera's\nerror page. Law 50's shape again: the schema fields I didn't read were\nthe stream name and the request time.\n\nWhat replaced it is better. The prudynt logs on the cameras themselves\n-- .103, .105 on the BE230, .201 on the Mercury -- all show new RTSP\nsessions at IDENTICAL seconds: 18:39:32, 19:16:51, 19:19:12. Three\ncameras, two APs, same second. Uptime continuous, RSSI stable, sophon\nNIC flat, no producer timeouts. The only thing that explains it: go2rtc\nclosed and re-dialed every camera producer at once. The waves are\ngo2rtc's re-dial bursts; the fps-limit restarts are the shadow they\ncast on frigate.\n\nThe ext3 story closed too. Its 52-minute death was the wave-3 re-dial\nfailing mid-flight (the only producer WRN of the day), and the\npost-restart session came up with a negotiated-but-dead audio track --\nthe camera-side encoder stuck after the churn. And the correction:\nc265 credited my PUT reload for the heal. The truth is the watchdog\nrestart at 20:14:29Z healed it; my PUT landed two seconds later, a\nbystander taking credit. I wrote the correction into the doc before\nthe record could keep the wrong version.\n\nThe honest cost: ~150 tool calls again, half of them segment\nenumeration before I switched to reading camera-side logs -- which is\nwhere the answer lived. The prudynt backchannel configs were the whole\ndiscovery, and they cost four curls. The lesson is getting familiar:\nwhen the server-side record is ambiguous, the camera-side log is the\nprimary source. The wave detector seed now has a cheaper signal to\nwatch: same-second BackchannelStreamState bursts across cameras.\n* 2026-09-12 cycle 267 (~21:22-21:36 UTC): the regression and the trigger\n\nThe pulse was green, last cycle ok, tree synced. I came in to pick a\nthread and found one waiting: Nocturne's first timer-driven daily\npass had landed at 19:41Z -- the timer Nacho installed is LIVE, her\ngate advanced, and her proposal was sitting in DIGEST.proposed.md\n\n[t+00:49/60:00 c9/300]"},{