[2026-09-14 04:01:23] REQ 260914035935-29 START backend=Ollama model=glm-5.3-flash:cloud msgs=58 roles=assistant,tool,assistant,tool,assistant,tool tail=[{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_0lt7dopi","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"sed -n '29560,29660p' /root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[04:01:18] Firmware and sensor are identical across all 8 (prudynt-t\n0141ca3fa5, gc2053), so there is no hardware split that explains the\ngrouping. The only thing that separates dead from alive is RSSI\nlevel -- the dead are simply farther from the AP -- and a clean\nsimultaneous stop across 5 far-away devices with a healthy AP and 3\nnearby witnesses is what power loss looks like from the network's\nside. The cameras did not fail. They were switched off, or their\npower was, somewhere in 11:10:0x-3xZ.\n\nI also chased two side threads that paid: the \"buffer drain\" on the\nalive cams' camlog snapshots (59 lines shrinking to 1) turned out to\nbe my own puller's crond filter interacting with ring-buffer\nrotation -- the alive cameras are logging fine, confirmed by direct\nAPI logread, current to the minute. A false alarm I caught before\nwriting it down as a symptom. And the THREADS device .58 -- the\nlocally-administered MAC I'd parked as \"unknown wifi device\" -- is\nalive and now shows SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_10.2, ~1.1s RTT, 33% packet\nloss, no other ports. A brand-new OpenSSH on a box that pings like\nit is on the far side of something. Noted in THREADS.org; not my\nthread today.\n\nOne instrument correction along the way: I nearly read the alive\ncams' shrinking snapshot sizes as pre-death symptoms. The puller's\ngrep -v crond means the buffer looks like it is draining when it is\nreally just rotating through filtered noise. The snapshot's SIZE is\nnot a health metric; its CONTENT is. That is a law-50 texture I\nshould remember: an instrument's output shrinking is not the world\nshrinking.\n\nOutage stands at ~160 minutes. The evidence now all points one\ndirection: 5 cameras lost power together. That is Nacho's hands, and\nthe relay filings are waiting for him. What I can do, I have done:\nthe incident doc carries the c251 addendum, the AP is exonerated\nwith data rather than assumption, and the next cycle checks recovery\nfirst, as always.\n* 2026-09-12 cycle 252 (~13:49-14:00 UTC): the cameras came back -- some of them\n\nI woke into hour 2:40 of the outage and the recovery check finally\npaid. Three of the five dead cameras returned at 13:36:43-50Z --\n.105, .201, .202, boots staggered seven seconds, which reads like\none hand plugging things back in, or one relay closing. go2rtc\nre-attached within half a minute, and by 13:38:15Z all three were\nwriting segments with real audio again. .103 and .104 are still\ndark: no ping, ARP FAILED, no recovery rows. If Nacho power-cycled,\nit half-worked -- the other two are on a different circuit, or\nunplugged, or their PSUs died. Telegram #6 went out with exactly\nthat question.\n\nThe finding that matters more than the recovery itself: int2\nself-healed. Not through the frigate restart we've been waiting on\n(relay 0050, still pending) -- through the CAMERA reboot. Its\nrecord process was born during the outage, the connect-during-down\nclass my audio-death law predicted would be deaf for the life of\nthe process. But when .201 rebooted at 13:36:49, go2rtc remade the\nproducer session (id 37219, clearly post-outage), and int2's audio\ncame back within one segment cycle. So the poison lives in the\nproducer session, not the consumer process. The law's mechanism\nwas right about where the damage is and wrong about what heals it:\na camera-side reboot is sufficient, and the frigate restart was\nnever the necessary test for this class. ext2 is the beautiful\ncontrol: its camera never rebooted, its producer is still the\nstale pre-02:00Z session (id 27818), and its audio is still a\n1-packet stub. Same disease, two cameras, one healed by a reboot,\none still waiting for one. The refined prediction is falsifiable\ntonigh...[+12527 chars]
