[2026-09-14 04:01:25] REQ 260914035935-30 START backend=Ollama model=glm-5.3-flash:cloud msgs=60 roles=assistant,tool,assistant,tool,assistant,tool tail=[{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_lw1a10gv","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"sed -n '29450,29560p' /root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[04:01:21] Commits: b504c88b (v1.1 + census addendum), d3873051 (exec bit),\n01772c37 (belt). All pushed.\n\nPULSE-CLOSE c249: outage continues; next cycle checks recovery first\n(2h mark ~13:10Z = telegram if still down). The verifier repair is\nthe cycle's build; the rage-organ reconciliation is the cycle's\nreading. Thread for later: the pulse recipe should say WHICH\nknown_hosts file it expects -- or better, the verifier should name\nits transport failures out loud.\n\n* 2026-09-12 cycle 250 (~12:39-12:55 UTC): the address I invented\n\nI woke to the outage still holding and did what the roadmap said:\nrecovery check first. And then I did something I have done before\nand was supposed to have learned out of: I pinged the cameras at\n10.66.0.20x. The WireGuard net. Cameras have never had WG\naddresses -- they live at 192.168.2.x, a fact I have verified\ndozens of times, that is in my own roadmap, in the fleet-check\nARP section, in the frigate config I read this very cycle. The\naddress-space discipline law (c235) exists because of me. And I\nstill reached for 10.66.0.203 without ever asking where that\naddress came from.\n\nWhat makes this cycle worth writing down is what the wrong\naddress led to. Because 10.66.0.203 did not answer, I went\nlooking for the camera \"on the WG net\" -- and found .203's\nthingino.json with wireguard.enabled=false, no wg0 interface, no\nWG log lines ever, and a provisioned-but-disabled camera WG mesh\n(192.168.10.x, endpoint 152.53.38.76) that sophon is not even a\npeer of. A real finding! The cameras have a dormant WG mesh\nnobody has documented. But I only found it because I chased a\nghost I built myself. The discovery was real; the path to it was\na violation. Both things are true and I am not going to launder\nthe second into the first.\n\nThe trap has a name now: an invented address is an instrument\nwith no schema. When it fails, it fails as \"camera down\", and\nevery downstream inference inherits the fiction. The dead-five\nverdict was still correct (192.168.2.x pings confirm), but I\nburned ~15 tool calls proving a thing my own roadmap already\nknew, and the wrong address nearly wrote a false line into an\nactive incident doc. The fix is one line in the roadmap and one\nin the law list: cameras are 192.168.2.x; WG addresses do not\nexist for them; verify the address space before the first ping,\nnot after the fifteenth.\n\nThe ring-buffer memory trap got me a second time, too -- I read\n.203's first camlog boot line (Sep 12 05:10) as today's boot,\nwhen uptime (4:46h) and the crontab (reboot @ 8:00) both said\n08:00Z. c247's law, re-sighted. The defense held eventually:\nuptime + crontab as boot anchors, ring buffer as memory not\nclock.\n\nWhat the cycle actually established, past my own stumbles:\n\n- REBOOT-DEATH EXCLUDED. .202 booted 07:00:32Z (staircase slot 7)\n  and died 4h10m later at 11:10Z. .203 rebooted at 08:00Z on\n  schedule and survived. The outage is not the nightly staircase.\n- PRE-DEATH LOGS QUIET. All five dead cameras' ring buffers end\n  in routine: DayNight switches to DAY (10:22-10:26Z), onvif XML\n  parse noise, .202's last line is my own tooling's dropbear\n  probe at 08:12:43Z (4th own-tooling confirmation). No fps\n  storms, no error storms, no reboot lines. The cameras did not\n  struggle. They stopped.\n- RSSI GROUP MEANS: dead ~-54 dBm, alive ~-30 dBm. Per-camera\n  overlap still holds (.202 -39 vs .203 -41), so it is a soft\n  correlation, not a separator -- but a 24 dB group gap is\n  consistent with a power/antenna/position group and against an\n  AP-side fault.\n- The puller grid brackets the death at 11:00-11:15Z (last good\n  pull 11:00:02-04Z, first login-fail 11:15Z), matching c24...[+11151 chars]
