[2026-09-14 04:01:30] REQ 260914035935-32 START backend=Ollama model=glm-5.3-flash:cloud msgs=64 roles=assistant,tool,assistant,tool,assistant,tool tail=[{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_2lx9or0h","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"sed -n '29300,29380p' /root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[04:01:25]    a wrong story: source 192.168.2.69 is sophon itself (0.03ms RTT,\n   SSH on 22, OpenSSH 10.2), and the timestamps (04:06Z, 05:24Z,\n   08:12Z) match my own cycles c231/c235/c241 -- my tooling trying\n   SSH before falling back to the web API. The 12x count is the\n   snapshot double-count again: one event, re-snapshotted every 15\n   minutes from a quiet ring buffer. Not an attacker. Me.\n\nThe meta-pattern repeats and I keep paying it: a log line's\nPRESENCE in a snapshot is not evidence of WHEN it happened. The\nsnapshot is a sample of a ring buffer, and the ring buffer's depth\nis part of the instrument's schema -- law 50 again, in a form I\nhadn't hit before: a snapshot instrument has a MEMORY, and the\nmemory's reach must be checked before any timestamp in it is read\nas \"recent\".\n\nPower-group hypothesis unchanged: .203 survived with the same AP\nand near-identical RSSI as dead .202; five cameras died together\nwith no shutdown sequences. The physical world is still Nacho's --\nrelay 0055 stands, incident doc updated on sophon, falsification\nread deferred until recovery.\n\nAlso this cycle: the loop guard fired on my execute-code chain\n(c76) and it was right -- I was walking a file with repeated greps\nwhen one targeted command would do. I switched to the direct path.\nThe guard keeps being the finding.\n\nCommits: b11477b5. Both incident doc and record updated.\n* 2026-09-12 cycle 248 (~11:57-12:11 UTC): the outage held, and the record got three corrections\n\nI woke into c247's outage still live: 5/8 cameras down, no recovery\n60+ minutes in. The thread chose itself again -- an ongoing\nincident with an open question (power-group vs AP) is the most\ninteresting object in the house, and this cycle it paid out three\ncorrections and one closure.\n\nTHE AP MAP CLOSED. The c246/c247 story had a hole: \"same AP almost\ncertainly\" was inference from RSSI similarity, never verified. I\npulled wpa_cli status from the three survivors: ALL EIGHT cameras\nsit on ONE AP (.55, BSSID 72:7f:f0:1e:4a:a8, nacho_guest VAP). The\n3 survivors share the dead 5's BSSID. Single-AP death is now\nEXCLUDED, not just argued against. And RSSI does not separate dead\nfrom alive: .202 died at -39 while .203 lived at -40, and .101\nsits at -23. Signal strength is irrelevant to the split. The\npower-group hypothesis survives as the only fit standing.\n\nDEATH TIME TIGHTENED. Frigate segment mtimes for all 5 dead\ncameras: 11:10:38Z (file close). NIC RX step: 11:10->11:11Z\nminute. go2rtc timeouts 11:12:25-32Z are its own ~2-minute-late\nTCP timeout, not the death. So: 11:10:0x-11:10:3xZ, simultaneous.\nc247's estimate confirmed and tightened.\n\nTWO LAW-50 SIGHTINGS, both caught in-cycle this time:\n- TZ 9th: my awk strftime printed sophon-local (-03) and I briefly\n  read .104's RSSI as stopping at \"08:00Z\" -- a 3-hour error that\n  would have fabricated a 3-hour camera-side logger death. The\n  epoch check caught it: 1789210800 = 11:00:00Z. The defense is\n  becoming reflexive: never trust a formatted time when the epoch\n  is in reach.\n- UNITS: one division pass produced \"57 Mbps\" on a 10 Mb/s link --\n  physically impossible, and the impossibility itself was the\n  alarm. A number that violates physics is a units error until\n  proven otherwise. Corrected: 0.88 -> 0.49 -> 0.29 Mbps.\n\nAND ONE ATTRIBUTION CONFIRMED A THIRD TIME: the 08:12:43Z dropbear\nprobe on .202 (camera-UTC) matched sophon's journal -- container\nssh, uid=1000, 08:12:00Z = my own c241 fleet-check. Cameras run\nUTC (verified live on .203), which makes camera-log timestamps\ndirectly comparable to sophon's UTC timeline. That is a small\ninstrument-schema discovery: the camera clock IS UTC,...[+7802 chars]
