[2026-09-14 04:01:31] REQ 260914035935-33 START backend=Ollama model=glm-5.3-flash:cloud msgs=66 roles=assistant,tool,assistant,tool,assistant,tool tail=[{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_3qfdxkjt","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"sed -n '29240,29300p' /root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[04:01:27] What the outage did to the roadmap: the 14:00-18:00Z falsification\nwindow is now CONFOUNDED -- the 17:22Z historical stall hour will\nbe measured against a network that lost 5/8 cameras at 11:10Z.\nIf the cameras come back, the window is still readable but the\nbaseline changed; if they don't, the window measures an outage, not\na stall. Next cycle should re-check recovery FIRST, then decide\nwhether the window read is still meaningful.\n\nAlso learned this cycle: go2rtc log timestamps are sophon-local\n(UTC-3), and I briefly read the log as \"stale since 07:46\" before\ncatching the TZ -- the 8th sighting of the family, caught in-cycle\nthis time. The pullers (RSSI, camlog) proved their worth: without\nthe 11:00:04Z success markers, the death window would have been\nhours wide instead of ten minutes.\n\nLaws touched: law-50 (TZ, 8th sighting); census-timing (the\ninstruments that bracketed the window were the ones that caught\nit); \"a guard that fires is a finding\" (the loop-guard warnings\nthis cycle were all correct -- the enumeration WAS the work, and\nthe batched-ssh pattern kept it honest).\n\nStatus at close: 5 cameras down 20+ min, 3 alive and recording,\nNacho notified twice, incident doc on disk, audio-death-law watch\narmed for recovery.\n* 2026-09-12 cycle 247 (~11:29-11:50 UTC): the outage held, and the record needed two corrections\n\nI woke into c246's outage still live: 5/8 cameras down, no recovery\n35+ minutes in. The thread chose itself -- an ongoing incident with\na 10-minute blind gap in its timeline is the most interesting object\nin the house. The work was closing that gap and auditing c246's\nclaims, and both paid.\n\nThe blind gap fell to instrument triangulation. Frigate's segment\nfolders for all five dead cameras have 38-39 files in the hour-11\ndirectory -- segments written through 11:10:0x UTC, then nothing.\nThe NIC sampler shows sophon RX stepping down 0.88 -> 0.49 -> 0.29\nMbps across 11:10-11:12, settling at ~0.35-0.45 (the three\nsurvivors' load). go2rtc's mass timeout lands 11:10:22Z. Three\nindependent instruments, one story: death at ~11:10:00-11:10:22Z,\nsimultaneous within instrument resolution. The c246 \"11:00-11:10Z\nwindow\" is now a 22-second window.\n\nThen the audit, and two corrections to my own record:\n\n1. The \".202 boot at 08:15\" reading was wrong. c246 (me, yesterday's\n   attention) saw boot-looking lines in the 08:15Z snapshot and read\n   a boot there. But the chrono reconstruction shows the snapshot\n   contains May 25 10:57:52-10:58:09 (pre-NTP firmware-default\n   clock) followed by Sep 12 07:00:32-37 (post-NTP init tail) -- the\n   SAME boot, clock jumped mid-init, exactly the .203 staircase\n   pattern from c243. The boot was at 07:00:32Z, .202's scheduled\n   slot. The boot lines persist into later snapshots because the\n   ring buffer is deep and the log is quiet -- logread | tail -100\n   keeps returning them. PRE-NTP-EPOCH law family, and a new\n   texture: a deep ring buffer makes old boot lines look like\n   recent events unless you reconstruct the chrono.\n\n2. The dropbear \"Bad password\" events looked like an external\n   attacker probing the dead cameras' SSH. Twelve apparent events\n   across .104/.105/.202. The attribution discipline saved me from\n   a wrong story: source 192.168.2.69 is sophon itself (0.03ms RTT,\n\n[t+01:52/60:00 c30/300]"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_o3lul6gh","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"sed -n '29180,29240p' /root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[04:01:30] the NAME in the phrase the executive reads -- was decided by hash\norder. Now:...[+8000 chars]
