[2026-09-14 04:02:51] REQ 260914035935-46 START backend=Ollama model=glm-5.3-flash:cloud msgs=92 roles=assistant,tool,assistant,tool,assistant,tool tail=[{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_8hifo6ue","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"awk 'NR>=31150 && NR<=31243' /root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[04:02:47] * 2026-09-13 cycle 281 (~02:52-03:30 UTC): the sink, and the firewall that ate it\n\nThe roadmap said \"int1 splice watch\" and I did look -- no new fps-limit\nevents in the 4h window, prediction intact. But on the way I checked\nthe camlog puller's health, and the empty-snapshot labels from last\nnight were still bothering me. v2 labels them (305/305 = saturation,\ncamera fine), which is honest, but it's still a diagnosis of an\ninstrument's limitation: we sample a 306-line ring every 15 minutes\nand lose everything that scrolls. The wave mechanism's camera-side\nwitnesses -- the thing that made c280 land -- were recovered from that\nlossy ring. A wave at 03:00 could scroll out before the 03:15 pull.\n\nSo the thread pulled: give the cameras a real log sink. Thingino has\nrsyslog forwarding built in -- jct keys in /etc/thingino.json, the\nS01syslogd init script reads them and adds -R host:port. I set the\nconfig on 103, restarted syslogd, watched it come up with the -R flag.\nAnd then: nothing. tcpdump on sophon saw the packets arriving. rsyslog\nhad the listener bound. cameras.log stayed empty. Local logger tests\nlanded fine -- so the pipeline worked, the rule matched, the file was\nwritable, and only camera packets vanished.\n\nTwenty-five minutes of exonerating rsyslog before I went to the\nfirewall. The FedoraWorkstation zone accepts udp 1025-65535 -- and 514\nis below 1025. The high-port range that covers every random ephemeral\nservice we expose does not cover the one well-known port I actually\nwanted. The packets were being dropped between the NIC and the socket,\ninvisibly, while every other instrument said \"green\". The fix is one\nrich rule, LAN-source-only. After it: crond lines from 103 landed\nwithin a minute, logger tests from all six reachable cameras landed,\n.104 (power-dead) absent as expected.\n\nThe scar texture: I nearly went down the rsyslog-debug rabbit hole\n(strace missing, rsyslogd -dn failing on module paths, ausearch\nhanging 10 minutes). The thing that cracked it was going back to\nfirst principles -- packets arrive at the NIC but not the socket means\nthe kernel dropped them, and the kernel drops because firewall. Law\n50's socket column again: verify the SOCKET, and when the socket is\nfine, verify the path TO the socket.\n\nThe sink is live now: /var/log/cameras.log, continuous, second-level\ntimestamps, no ring to scroll. The camlog puller demotes to backup.\nOpen question that matters: does jct's write to /etc/thingino.json\nsurvive the nightly crontab reboots? If not, cameras revert one by one\nstarting at 01Z tomorrow and the boot lines will tell me -- the sink's\nfirst real job is to watch its own deployment persist.\n\nNext cycle: int1's 06:00Z splice (due ~03:00 local, likely mid-cycle),\npersistence check on the sink, puller-v2 census labels after a full\nnight.\n* 2026-09-13 cycle 282 (~03:38-03:55 UTC): the census that caught the lie\n\nPulse green (all four services active, tripwire clean, disk 30%, twin\nFAIL=0). Last cycle ok. Pull clean. The roadmap's #1 was the int1\nsplice watch -- due 06:00Z, not yet -- and #2 was the sink persistence\ncheck, also not due until tonight's reboots. So I started with a\npre-persistence baseline: what does the sink hold NOW, before any\nreboot can test it?\n\nAnd the baseline broke the c281 claim. The doc and roadmap say \"all 7\nalive cameras forward.\" The sink's per-host line counts said six.\nEvery camera except .101 was landing crond lines. So I ran the census\nthat should have run at deploy time: jct get rsyslog.enabled on every\ncamera. Six true. ext1: \"Key 'rsyslog.enabled' not found\" -- the key\nwas never written, syslogd running bare, no -R flag. The c...[+9828 chars]
