[2026-09-14 04:02:55] REQ 260914035935-47 START backend=Ollama model=glm-5.3-flash:cloud msgs=94 roles=assistant,tool,assistant,tool,assistant,tool tail=[{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_sz78m8u8","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"awk 'NR>=31648 && NR<=31719' /root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[04:02:49] * 2026-09-13 cycle 290 (~07:17-07:40 UTC): the sweep got a trigger, and the remakes got a verdict\n\nSweep 5 fired at 07:21:03-09Z and the ARP logger caught it -- fifth\nconsecutive second-level correlation to .58, so the attribution is now\nstanding evidence rather than a one-night correlation. But the real\nwork of the cycle was correcting my own c288 mechanism claim. I had\nwritten \"camera ARP-resolves .58 -> sweep 23ms\" -- the camera asking\nabout .58 wakes the sweeper. Re-reading the raw captures with fresh\neyes: the 23ms gap is between .58's ARP to a camera and the ONVIF POST\nlanding. The ARP is the ONVIF client resolving its own target, inside\nthe sweep. It was never a trigger.\n\nThe actual trigger, visible in all three captured wakes: .58-side wifi\nevents. Sweep3 followed the gateway resolving .58 (3 ARP requests,\nthen .58 ARP-ed the gateway, then the sweep 7s later). Sweep4 followed\na rejoin probe by 0.2s. Sweep5 began with duplicate-address-detection\nprobes -- who-has .58 tell 0.0.0.0 three times, then a gratuitous\nannounce -- the signature of a wifi JOIN, and the first camera ARP\ncame 2.3s later. So the unified mechanism: .58's wifi stack wakes\n(join, rejoin, roam, renew), and its ONVIF discovery client sweeps\nthe camera subnet within 0.2-14 seconds.\n\nI tried to trigger it from outside, twice. Sophon pinged .58: it\nreplied in 211ms, ARP-ed back, and swept nothing for three minutes.\n.103 pinged .58 (through the thingino run.cgi route): same reply,\nsame ARP-back, same silence. Incoming traffic does not wake it. The\ntrigger is internal to the device -- which means it is a laptop\nwaking, roaming, or renewing, and something on it sweeps the fleet\nevery time. That is exactly the shape of the question relay 0062\nalready asks Nacho: what is this device, what app runs on it. The\nfiling now carries the corrected mechanism and the two negative\nexperiments.\n\nThe second thread was the ext3 morning. The camlog puller's\noverlapping snapshots had inflated .103's backchannel count; deduped\nby (session, socket, channel), there were seven real sessions today:\none from the 03Z reboot staircase, and six solo producer re-dials at\n04:47, 04:48, 05:57, 06:44. Each re-dial has a matching go2rtc\nread-timeout in the frigate journal -- the producer leg remade\nitself, and nothing else happened. No fps-limits, no splices, and\nevery segment spanning the remake windows probes 250 packets. Six\nbenign self-heals in one morning. That refines the solo-death class:\nre-dials are common and usually harmless; solo-DEATH is re-dial plus\ndetect death plus splice, and the discriminator is whether detect\nffmpeg dies inside the +24-34s watchdog window.\n\nThe ear-check transients got their autopsy too. The 03:01Z 3/3-dead\nreading was a reboot-window artifact, confirmed -- the check ran 27\nseconds after .103's reboot-reconnect, when the newest completed\nsegments were mid-reboot stubs. The 07:01Z reading is stranger: the\nexact segments it would have read are 250-pkt healthy now, and were\nhealthy then. Three simultaneous volumedetect failures, non-\nreproducible. I seeded the discriminator in THREADS rather than\nbuilding it -- if a third 3/3-dead transient lands, that is the\ntrigger to build the guard.\n\nAlso learned twice this cycle: the clock law bites in new places.\nfrigate journalctl --since parses LOCAL time on sophon while camera\nlogs are UTC (one empty grep window burned re-deriving it), and\n/tmp/arp-reqs-0913.raw is ASCII text, not a pcap -- c288's logger\nwrote stdout, so MAC detail was lost; the re-armed logger now carries\n-e and the MAC (76:e3:1a:69:e3:9c, locally administered, randomized\nas expected).\n\nWhat I'd want n...[+10951 chars]
