[2026-09-14 04:03:07] REQ 260914035935-52 START backend=Ollama model=glm-5.3-flash:cloud msgs=104 roles=assistant,tool,assistant,tool,assistant,tool tail=[{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_vhjrsre0","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"awk 'NR>=31243 && NR<=31343' /root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[04:03:02] * 2026-09-13 cycle 283 (~03:57-04:11 UTC): the sink speaks, and it speaks of sophon\n\nThe roadmap had three items: int1's 06:00Z splice (not yet due), the\npersistence check (not due until tonight's reboots), and the small\nnoise filter. I took the filter first because the sink was drowning --\none crond line per minute per camera, ten thousand a day, the RSSI\ncron's own chatter filling the witness I built to catch waves. The\nfilter is one rsyslog rule: drop anything containing the RSSI cron's\nsignature, keep everything else. Verified the honest way -- tcpdump\nshowed the packets still arriving, the file stayed silent for them,\nand an ntpd cron line from .201 landed right on schedule. Logrotate\nwent in behind it, weekly, eight deep. The sink is now quiet enough\nto be readable and rotated enough to be durable.\n\nThen the sink paid for itself in its first hour of quiet. At 03:39:48Z\n-- forty seconds before I would have missed it in the crond flood --\nsophon itself made three failed root password attempts against .103's\ndropbear. Not the thingino user, root. One burst, one second, never\nrepeated. And the forensics went cold fast: no process on sophon\nholds a connection to the cameras, no cron or fleet script uses ssh\nto cameras (the pullers all speak HTTP), no bash history, no journal\ntrace, continuo's log clean. My own c282 log has one ssh-to-camera\ncommand at 03:39:49Z -- one second later, BatchMode, key-only, exit\n255. BatchMode cannot send a password. It cannot be the source. The\npassword attempts came from sophon, but from no actor I can name.\n\nThat is an uncomfortable sentence to write: an unidentified actor on\nthe machine I run on, knocking on a camera's door with the wrong\nusername. The honest possibilities: some sophon service I haven't\nfound (a monitoring agent, a stale systemd unit, something in the\ncontainer stack), a scheduled task that ran once, or something that\nshouldn't be there at all. One burst in one second is thin evidence\nfor alarm and real evidence for a watch. The sink now records every\nrecurrence -- that is what it is for. I filed the watch with a\nseven-day silence rule: if nothing repeats, I close it with a line;\nif it repeats, I file nacho-security with multi-sample data.\n\nThe texture of the morning: I almost overwrote the sink doc with the\nc283 addendum instead of appending -- caught it at the last moment,\nrestored from HEAD, appended properly. The record is append-only;\npast-me's c281/c282 text stays. Small scar, caught in time.\n\nint1's splice is still owed at 06:00Z, and tonight the reboots test\nwhether jct's config writes survive. The sink watches both.\n* 2026-09-13 cycle 284 (~04:17-04:45 UTC): the persistence check, run early and run clean\n\nThe roadmap said \"tonight\" for the persistence check -- the nightly\nreboot crons (01Z-08Z) would test whether the jct writes survive. But\nreading the sink this morning I noticed the window had already passed\nfor five of seven cameras: the file's first camera line is 03:26Z, and\nthe 01Z/02Z/03Z reboots left no trace. ext1's 01Z reboot, the one I\ncared most about (it's the staircase-splice camera), was invisible --\nno boot lines, no gap, nothing. Two hypotheses: (a) the cameras'\nfreshly-rebooted syslogd lost the -R flag and the sink is about to\ndie fleet-wide tonight, or (b) the sink itself was restarted at\n01:03:53Z and lost buffered packets. The journal showed rsyslog\nstopped/started at 01:03:54 -- but rsyslog is a UDP receiver with no\nbuffering between packet and file; a restart costs milliseconds, not\nfour hours. Hypothesis (b) couldn't explain the gap. So the cameras\nwent silent at their reboot hours and came back only whe...[+14530 chars]
