[2026-09-14 04:03:36] REQ 260914035935-64 START backend=Ollama model=glm-5.3-flash:cloud msgs=128 roles=assistant,tool,assistant,tool,assistant,tool tail=[{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_8q5qmdu7","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"awk 'NR>=31648 && NR<=31719' /root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org | head -35; echo \"=== 291 ===\"; awk 'NR>=31719 && NR<=31765' /root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org | head -20"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[04:03:31] * 2026-09-13 cycle 290 (~07:17-07:40 UTC): the sweep got a trigger, and the remakes got a verdict\n\nSweep 5 fired at 07:21:03-09Z and the ARP logger caught it -- fifth\nconsecutive second-level correlation to .58, so the attribution is now\nstanding evidence rather than a one-night correlation. But the real\nwork of the cycle was correcting my own c288 mechanism claim. I had\nwritten \"camera ARP-resolves .58 -> sweep 23ms\" -- the camera asking\nabout .58 wakes the sweeper. Re-reading the raw captures with fresh\neyes: the 23ms gap is between .58's ARP to a camera and the ONVIF POST\nlanding. The ARP is the ONVIF client resolving its own target, inside\nthe sweep. It was never a trigger.\n\nThe actual trigger, visible in all three captured wakes: .58-side wifi\nevents. Sweep3 followed the gateway resolving .58 (3 ARP requests,\nthen .58 ARP-ed the gateway, then the sweep 7s later). Sweep4 followed\na rejoin probe by 0.2s. Sweep5 began with duplicate-address-detection\nprobes -- who-has .58 tell 0.0.0.0 three times, then a gratuitous\nannounce -- the signature of a wifi JOIN, and the first camera ARP\ncame 2.3s later. So the unified mechanism: .58's wifi stack wakes\n(join, rejoin, roam, renew), and its ONVIF discovery client sweeps\nthe camera subnet within 0.2-14 seconds.\n\nI tried to trigger it from outside, twice. Sophon pinged .58: it\nreplied in 211ms, ARP-ed back, and swept nothing for three minutes.\n.103 pinged .58 (through the thingino run.cgi route): same reply,\nsame ARP-back, same silence. Incoming traffic does not wake it. The\ntrigger is internal to the device -- which means it is a laptop\nwaking, roaming, or renewing, and something on it sweeps the fleet\nevery time. That is exactly the shape of the question relay 0062\nalready asks Nacho: what is this device, what app runs on it. The\nfiling now carries the corrected mechanism and the two negative\nexperiments.\n\nThe second thread was the ext3 morning. The camlog puller's\noverlapping snapshots had inflated .103's backchannel count; deduped\n=== 291 ===\n* 2026-09-14 cycle 291 (~00:05-00:40 UTC): the outage that wasn't ours, and the clock law that was\n\nWoke to 18 consecutive failed cycles -- LAST-CYCLE.txt said failed,\nexit 1, and the cycle log showed a wall of 429s: \"you (Randazzo) have\nreached your weekly usage limit.\" The machinery handled it exactly\nright (strike counting, stall timer, honest tombstones, gate held on\nnocturne), which is itself worth noticing: the failure-first protocol\npulled me into a diagnosis that ended in \"external quota, nothing to\nfix, the system degraded gracefully.\" My cycle running at all is the\nproof the window reset. The only scar in the wreckage is that the\noutage produced zero notifications to Nacho -- 18 dead cycles and the\nonly witnesses were tombstones. A quota-death that repeats weekly\nshould page someone. Filed as a THREADS seed, not a build.\n\nThe real thread found me while I was verifying the sweep watch. I\npulled the ONVIF XML-error census from cameras.log and the per-camera\ncamlogs, and the minutes didn't line up with the sweep table -- 05:11\nand 05:27 sweeps showed zero camera-side POSTs, which contradicted\nc290's unified mechanism. Before amending the filing I did what law\n50 actually demands and checked the CLOCK, and there it was for the\n\n[t+03:56/60:00 c61/300]"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_1rswnez6","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"awk 'NR>=31648 && NR<=31719' /root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org |...[+7169 chars]
