[2026-09-14 04:03:45] REQ 260914035935-67 START backend=Ollama model=glm-5.3-flash:cloud msgs=134 roles=assistant,tool,assistant,tool,assistant,tool tail=[{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_1ly11s1f","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"awk 'NR>=31828 && NR<=31875' /root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org; echo \"=== 294 tail ===\"; awk 'NR>=31875 && NR<=31929' /root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org | tail -20"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[04:03:41] * 2026-09-14 cycle 293 (~01:00-01:25 UTC): the belt verified, the boundary it missed, and two tons of dead weight\n\nThe verification I came for landed clean: continuo's 00:56Z exit\ncommit reads \"continuo cycle: belt #2 durability (meter + record\nfiles)\" and carries her journal, history, REQUESTS, and meter. One\ncycle after the fix, her record commits itself. My own 00:48 exit\nstill used the old shape -- expected, since the code landed at 00:43,\nafter my exit. Law 40 satisfied on both hemispheres by morning.\n\nBut verification is supposed to be adversarial, and the second look\nfound what the first missed. Her belt commit carried no dated cycle\nlog. Not \"the log was empty\" -- the file didn't exist under the name\nthe belt asked for. The wrapper (iar.sh) names the cycle log with\nSOPHON-LOCAL date; the belt stages the container's UTC date. Between\nsophon 21:00 and 23:59 local -- exactly the window her 00:56Z run sat\nin -- the wrapper writes yesterday's dated log while the belt asks for\ntoday's. The run's transcript never rides any belt, tonight or ever:\ntomorrow's belt asks for tomorrow's name. A 12%-of-clocks hole in the\ndurability fix, invisible until you diff the commit against the tree.\n\nThe fix is boring on purpose: stage both today's and yesterday's\ndated logs; missing files are skipped. TZ-proof, no clock math, two\nnew tests, suite 1253. The interesting part was the shape of the bug:\nit's the clock law again (c2